<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War · Crisis</p><h1 class="enc-title">War of the Jülich Succession</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1609">1609</a>–<a href="/years/1614">1614</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Jülich succession crisis · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War of the Jülich Succession was a dispute over the United Duchies of Jülich-Cleves-Berg after the death of the childless Duke John William in 1609. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatinate-neuburg">Palatinate-Neuburg</a>, backed by Protestant and Catholic allies, contested the inheritance, which nearly set off a general European war. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-xanten">Treaty of Xanten</a> of 1614 divided the lands, giving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>, Mark and Ravensberg to Brandenburg.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-of-the-julich-succession.jpg" alt="War of the Jülich Succession" width="320" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gankbank789 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:War_of_the_J%C3%BClich_Succession.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By the early 17th century the spread of Lutheran and Calvinist teaching had met the Catholic resurgence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>, and conflicts such as the Cologne War and the Strasbourg Bishops' War had produced two armed camps, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-league">Catholic League</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-union">Protestant Union</a>. The United Duchies of Jülich-Cleves-Berg covered some 14,000 km², lay close to the Spanish Road and had a booming economy, fed by refugees from the Eighty Years' War. Duke John William, who suffered from serious mental illness, died without heirs on 25 March 1609.</p><p>The Protestant claimants were Johann Sigismund, Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, and Wolfgang Wilhelm of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatinate-neuburg">Palatinate-Neuburg</a>, whose small principality, hemmed in by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and the Electoral Palatinate, saw the rich duchies as a major prize. The Catholic Archduke Leopold V occupied Jülich.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The first phase ran from 10 June 1609 to 24 October 1610. Leopold was left without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> money or troops, apart from 6,000 escudos sent secretly by the Spanish ambassador Baltasar de Zúñiga. Henry IV of France backed the claimants, perhaps moved also by the flight of the Prince and Princess of Condé to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>. At Schwäbisch Hall, from 10 January 1610, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-union">Protestant Union</a> agreed that the claimants would raise 5,000 infantry and 1,300 cavalry, the Union 4,000 infantry and 1,000 cavalry, and Henry 8,000 infantry and 2,000 cavalry. Leopold was defeated, and the Treaty of Dortmund set up a temporary joint rule.</p><p>The two possessors then fell out. Wolfgang Wilhelm became a Catholic, Johann Sigismund announced his conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a> on Christmas Day 1613, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>'s new viceroy, George William, proved popular with the Jülich nobility. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>, Protestants had expelled the Catholic council in a riot on 5 July 1611. In May 1614 300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> troops ejected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuburg-an-der-donau">Neuburg</a> garrison from Jülich, and Wolfgang Wilhelm answered by seizing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a>. Ambrogio Spinola led about 20,000 men to Aachen, besieged it from 24 August 1614 and restored the old council.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Spinola and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-prince-of-orange">Maurice of Nassau</a> opened peace talks on 13 October 1614, with Spanish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> envoys standing for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuburg-an-der-donau">Neuburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and mediators from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The resulting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-xanten">Treaty of Xanten</a> favoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> but did not settle the matter. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> the duchies saw the Siege of Jülich of 1621-1622 and were burdened by Swedish, Imperial and Hessian troops, and the rivalry of Neuburg and Brandenburg flared again in the Düsseldorf Cow War. The crisis drew in Spain, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, France and England and is counted among the European wars of religion.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “War of the Jülich Succession” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>Jülich succession crisis</b>, the Treaty of Xanten of 1614 assigned Ravensberg to Brandenburg, confirmed in 1666.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-ravensberg">County of Ravensberg</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Jülich succession crisis</b>, the Treaty of Xanten in 1614 gave Cleves to Brandenburg, giving the Hohenzollerns their first foothold in the west.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-cleves">Duchy of Cleves</a></li><li><blockquote>When the last duke, John William, died in 1609, the <b>Jülich succession crisis</b> between Brandenburg and Palatinate-Neuburg nearly sparked a European war.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-julich">Duchy of Jülich</a></li><li><blockquote>The mentally ill Duke John William died childless in 1609, setting off the <b>War of the Jülich Succession</b> between Brandenburg and Palatinate-Neuburg.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-duchies-of-julich-cleves-berg">United Duchies of Jülich-Cleves-Berg</a></li></ul></section>
